   "text": {
    "L1": "Touch can mislead us about how hot or cold something is.",
    "L2": "A hand from cold water feels lukewarm water as warm, while a warm hand feels the same water as cool.",
    "L3": "So we use a thermometer to measure temperature, usually in degrees Celsius, or °C.",
    "L4": "Steel and wood spoons in the same room can be at the same temperature.",
    "L5": "Steel feels colder because metal takes heat away from our hand faster than wood.",
    "L6": "Temperature tells how hot something is. Heat is energy flowing from a hotter object to a colder one, and they are not the same.",
    "L7": "On the Celsius scale, water freezes at 0 °C, normal body temperature is about 37 °C, and water boils at about 100 °C.",
    "L8": "Now put these Celsius temperatures in order from lowest to highest."
   },
